  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA deepening surface low is tracking across the Great Lakes and \ndriving due east while dragging a sharp cold front across the \narea today. The environment will be a high shear and low \ninstability type environment with strong DCAPE and anomously \nhigh PWATs. Widespread severe weather is not anticipated \nhowever strong forced ascent along the front will allow for \nheavier shower activity and a few isolated thunderstorms to \ntransport stronger momentum aloft down to the surface. A \nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms remains to our northeast\nwith isolated damaging winds being the primary threat. Steering\nflow will be strong enough to force shower or thunderstorm \nactivity along without causing too much in the way of possible\nflooding issues. \n\nOutside thunderstorm activity strong cold air advection and a \ntightening pressure gradient will yield widepsread wind gusts \nof 25 to 35mph across the lowlands and across the higher \nterrain gusts up to 40mph+ are probable as the front clears out\nthis afternoon. Strong synoptic gusty winds will continue into \nearly Saturday afternoon. \n\nTemperatures will promote a non-diurnal day and after mid \nmorning starting in the upper 60s and 70s temperatures will \ncrash into the 50s from west to east by late afternoon following\nfrontal passage. Residual low-level moisture and upslope \nnorthwesterly flow will keep clouds and light showers lingering \ninto Friday night and Saturday morning, especially on the \nwestern slopes of the mountains. Cold advection is strong \nenough that a few snowflakes may mix in at the highest peaks \naround Snowshoe, WV by daybreak Saturday. Accumulations are not \nexpected at this time as probabilities are lower than 10% of \nthat occurring. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBy Saturday afternoon, precipitation ends and high pressure \nbuilds in from the west effectively scouring out clouds and \nrelaxing the pressure gradient. This will set up a very optimal\nradiational cooling night for Saturday into Sunday morning. \nWith clear skies and decoupling winds along with a cold modified\nairmass firmly in place, temperatures will be able to plummet \nsignificantly. NBM deterministic and probabilistic guidance \nstrongly indicates lows falling into the upper 20s to low 30s \nacross the board. \n\nProbabilities for lower than 32 degrees exceeded 90% for \ntraditional cold spots and sheltered valleys in southeast Ohio \nand along the mountains. The broader lowlands of the Tri-state \narea suggests a 60-80% chances of seeing a hard freeze. \nTherefore, widespread frost and freeze conditions are expected \nlikely ending the growing season for much of the area. Dense \nfreezing river valley fog may also develop in the deeper river \nbasins early Sunday and monday mornings. \n\nAfter collaboration with neighboring offices we all decided to \nhold off on a freeze watch for now. Neighbors did not want to go\nwith one just yet so day shift will have to look at it and \nstart a collaboration again. Regardless, we will need headlines \nfor Saturday nights frost/freeze that is expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHeaded in to next week probabilities of precipitation near zero\ncontinuing an abnormally dry period in the long term as high\npressure builds into the region and does not show signs of\nretreating. As an upper level trough exits to the east a broad\nmid level ridge and strong surface high pressure migrate from \nthe Midwest to our region. This will yield a steady warming \ntrend bringing daytime highs back to the upper 60s and low 70s \nby midweek with pleasant dry conditions expected.",
